date = 2026-08-04
description = "Cloudflare ships an open-source AI app-generation platform you can deploy yourself — onto their edge. Doing the same on your own cluster is not an AI problem, it is a tenancy problem, and the isolation an agent run needs is almost exactly what a tenant namespace already gives you. Here is the mapping, the capacity arithmetic on a measured cluster, and the parts I would not build."
series = "self-hosting"
part = 7
+++

A software factory, in the sense I mean it here: a service that takes a brief,
spawns a sandboxed coding agent per variant, lets it write an application,
verifies the result, and reports back.

Generating the application is the easy part. The moment it works you are
running untrusted code that a language model wrote, at scale, on hardware you
pay for. That is a sandboxing and resource-isolation problem before it is an AI
problem.

It also maps, almost line for line, onto per-tenant isolation — which means if
you already have [a k3s platform with real tenancy](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/),
you have most of it. The parts you do not have are a deadline and a guaranteed
teardown, and those are the two the agents actually need.

::: note
**Part 7 of 7 of [Running things on your own hardware](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

Previous: [What a game world server actually needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)
:::

